{"id": 9503928, "name": "STANTON v. SOUTHERN RY.", "name_abbreviation": "Stanton v. Southern Ry.", "decision_date": "1900-01-03", "docket_number": "", "first_page": "398", "last_page": "399", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "56 S.C. 398"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "54 S. C., 599", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4409388], "pin_cites": [{"page": "90"}], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/54/0599-01"]}, {"cite": "53 S. C., 123", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "46 S. C., 203", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2348666], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/46/0203-01"]}, {"cite": "35 S. C., 34", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "33 S. C., 88", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "pin_cites": [{"page": "583"}], "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "29 S. C., 4", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4389838], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/29/0004-01"]}, {"cite": "28 S. C., 29", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4389374], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/28/0029-01"]}, {"cite": "26 S. C., 199", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "94 Wis., 270", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Wis.", "case_ids": [8703919], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/wis/94/0270-01"]}, {"cite": "26 S. C., 168", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "29 S. C., 152", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4390121], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/29/0152-01"]}, {"cite": "36 S. C., 375", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "26 S. C., 302", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 221, "char_count": 2769, "ocr_confidence": 0.734, "pagerank": {"raw": 1.0914529428872865e-07, "percentile": 0.5657134836818671}, "sha256": "71fad5b240535c3b5c6c8a4957534124ac5c8d4eaf73ce74b0597c394751ec0d", "simhash": "1:89a08ace9f14b790", "word_count": 480}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T17:46:48.748667+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["STANTON v. SOUTHERN RY."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion o\u00ed the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Gary.\nThis was an action for damages caused by fire communicated by defendant\u2019s locomotive, and injuring plaintiff\u2019s lands.\nThe jury rendered a verdict in favor of the plaintiff, and the defendant appealed upon the following exceptions : \u201cThe defendant, in pursuance of the notice of appeal heretofore served in the above entitled cause, hereby excepts because the presiding Judge refused to charge the second request of the defendant, to wit: \u2018If the evidence raises a mere conjecture or a suspicion, or even a probability, that the fire was caused 'by the locomotive engine of the defendant, that is not sufficient to- enable the plaintiff to recover. The probability must amount to proof.\u201d The charge of the presiding Judge shows that he practically charged what was contained in the request, and the appellant was, therefore, not prejudiced by such refusal. There is, however, another reason why the exception cannot be sustained. If the defendant had omitted the word \u201cprobability\u201d in the request, the Circuit Judge could.very properly have charged it; but with that word in the request, he could not have charged it without invading the province of the jury. The inference to be drawn from testimony depends in every case, more or less, on probability. The preponderance of the evidence may depend upon the probability, and if the Circuit Judge had undertaken to instruct the jury as to the effect of probability, he would have commented on the facts and invaded the province of the jury. The Circuit Judge was not bound to charge a request unless the propositions of law therein were sound in their entirety. He was not 'bound, as has been held time and again, to charge so much of a request as contained sound propositions of law. The exceptions must be overruled.\nIt is the judgment of this Court, that the judgment of the Circuit Court be affirmed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Gary."}], "attorneys": ["Messrs. .Iiw. P. Thomas, Jr., and B. L. Abney, for appel-lanQ.'", "Messrs. RagsdaZe & Ragsdale, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "STANTON v. SOUTHERN RY.\nCHARGE-TO instruct a jury that \"If the evidence raises a mere conj ect-tire or suspicion, or even a probability\" as to the contention of plaintiff, would be error, because the inference or preponderance of evidence may depend on probability.\nBefore GARY, J., Fairfield, February Term, 1899.\nAffirmed.\nAction by D. H. Stanton against Southern Railway Co. for damages to land by fire. From judgment on verdict for plaintiff, defendant appeals.\nMessrs. .Iiw. P. Thomas, Jr., and B. L. Abney, for appel-lanQ.'\nThe former cites on question raised: 26 S. C., 302; 36 S. C., 375; 29 S. C., 152; 26 S. C., 168; i\u00e7, S. C., 39; 94 Wis., 270.\nMessrs. RagsdaZe & Ragsdale, contra,\ncite on point raised: 26 S. C., 199; 28 S. C., 29; 29 S. C., 4; 33 S. C., 88, 583; 35 S. C., 34; 46 S. C., 203; 53 S. C., 123; 54 S. C., 599, 90.\nJanuary 3, 1900."}, "file_name": "0398-01", "first_page_order": 406, "last_page_order": 407}